(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-copd-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 61-year-old man who smokes has coughed up thick sputum most days for 4 months of each of the last 3 years. He has rhonchi, bluish lips and swelling of both ankles. His blood gas shows low oxygen and raised carbon dioxide, and his diffusing capacity is near normal. Post-bronchodilator spirometry shows a ratio of 0.64. Which subtype best describes his disease?","choices":["Chronic bronchitis subtype","Emphysema subtype","Chronic bronchitis without obstruction","Asthma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Productive cough for at least 3 months a year over 2 years, rhonchi, cyanosis, edema, early hypoxemia and hypercapnia, and a near-normal diffusing capacity, with confirmed obstruction (ratio 0.64), are the chronic bronchitis picture (the blue bloater). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 14, 15"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with minimal smoking history has emphysema with a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.61. His chest x-ray shows the changes mainly in the lower lobes. Which test is most appropriate to find the cause?","choices":["B-type natriuretic peptide","Alpha-1 antitrypsin level and genotype","Computed tomography of the chest","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients under 45, or without significant exposure, should be evaluated for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, and basilar emphysema adds to the suspicion. A level below 11 micromol/L with a severely deficient genotype is diagnostic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 10, 19, 30"},{"q":"A 75-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a severe exacerbation. She is confused, her blood pressure is falling despite fluids, and she is tiring and may need invasive ventilation. Which level of care is most appropriate?","choices":["Outpatient management","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Intensive care admission","Hospital ward admission"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intensive care admission is for patients who are hemodynamically unstable, have a major infection or need invasive ventilatory support, and for sepsis, altered mental status, respiratory failure or respiratory acidosis. Her confusion, falling pressure and possible need for intubation meet these. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman reports severe breathlessness, stopping after about 100 meters on level ground, but her post-bronchodilator forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 82% predicted with a ratio of 0.68. Her heart evaluation is normal. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Long-term oxygen","Referral to pulmonology","Triple inhaled therapy","Reassurance with no follow-up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her dyspnea grade of 3 is far worse than her near-normal airflow (grade 1, 82%) would explain. Symptoms out of proportion to pulmonary function tests, like diagnostic uncertainty, is a listed reason for pulmonology referral. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 24, 34, 54"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease who smokes is asked about his smoking and is clearly advised to quit, with a link to his diabetes and heart risk. Using the Five A's approach, which step should come next?","choices":["Assist with a quit plan","Ask about his smoking again","Assess his readiness to change","Arrange a follow-up visit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After Ask and Advise, the Five A's continue with Assess, evaluating the person's readiness and willingness to change, then Assist with actionable steps, and Arrange follow-up. Readiness shapes how the clinician helps. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has had 2 falls and a brief fainting episode this year without a cardiac rhythm problem on monitoring. Which explanation, listed among the disease's secondary effects, fits best?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroid toxicity","Allergic reaction to her inhaler","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Poor perfusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients may have symptoms secondary to the disease, including syncopal episodes or falls due to poor perfusion, as well as coronary artery disease and right heart strain. Older adults also face frailty, falls and cognitive decline. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 17, 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 18 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 2. She has had no exacerbations in the past year. Under the current three-group scheme, which group is she in?","choices":["Group D","Group B","Group A","Group E"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 18 (10 or more) and a dyspnea grade of 2 (2 or more) mean more symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk. More symptoms with low risk is group B, which starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is taught to breathe out slowly through pursed lips when she is short of breath. She asks why it helps. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It slows her heart rate","It increases inspiratory flow","It reduces mucus production","It keeps her airways open"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pursed-lip breathing increases intra-airway pressure, which keeps the airways open during expiration and allows more gas exchange. It counters the collapse of airways that loss of elastic recoil causes in emphysema. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 85% on room air on 2 visits, with no cor pulmonale (right heart failure). Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Oxygen just during exercise","No oxygen until saturation falls below 80%","A sleep study for apnea","Long-term oxygen for over 15 hours a day"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe resting desaturation, a saturation of 88% or less at rest, qualifies for long-term oxygen therapy for more than 15 hours a day. Oxygen for severe resting hypoxemia decreases the risk of death, prolongs life and improves quality of life. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 63, 64, 66"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease develops sudden sharp chest pain and severe breathlessness while coughing. Breath sounds are absent over the right chest. Before treating this as an exacerbation, which test best excludes the diagnosis most likely here?","choices":["Alpha-1 antitrypsin level","6-minute walk test","Chest x-ray","Spirometry with a bronchodilator"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pneumothorax is one of the diagnoses to exclude before calling a decline an exacerbation, and the chest x-ray, though not a diagnostic standard for the disease itself, can rule out other diagnoses such as heart failure or pneumothorax. Sudden pain with absent breath sounds points there. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 56"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease had 2 moderate exacerbations and 1 hospital admission in the past year. His blood eosinophil count is 350. He is starting maintenance therapy. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Daily oral corticosteroids","Triple inhaled therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two moderate exacerbations and an admission make him high risk, group E. Group E starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic, and adding an inhaled corticosteroid is considered when blood eosinophils are 300 or more, as his 350 are. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 42"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 24 and a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 3. He had 3 moderate exacerbations in the past year. Using the 2020 four-group scheme, which group is he in?","choices":["Group E","Group D","Group B","Group C"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A symptom score of 24 and a dyspnea grade of 3 mean more symptoms, and 3 moderate exacerbations mean high risk. In the 2020 scheme, more symptoms with high risk is group D (group E in the current scheme). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 41"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease says that, walking on level ground, he has to stop for breath after about 100 meters or after a few minutes. Which modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 3 is stopping for breath after walking about 100 meters or after a few minutes on the level. A grade of 2 or more counts as more symptoms, which places him in group B or E depending on exacerbation risk. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 45% predicted, a 6-minute walk distance of 200 meters, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 3, and a body mass index of 20. What is his BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index score?","choices":["5","6","7","8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 1-second volume 45% (36 to 49) = 2 + walk 200 meters (150 to 249) = 2 + dyspnea grade 3 = 2 + body mass index 20 (21 or less) = 1, for a total of 7. A score of 7 to 10 predicts about 18% 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is reviewed for comorbidities that become more important with age. Which condition should be added to his screening at this age?","choices":["Osteoporosis and falls","Allergic rhinitis","Exercise-induced bronchospasm","Nasal polyps"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Comorbidities in older adults add osteoporosis, frailty, falls and cognitive decline to cardiovascular disease, metabolic disease and depression. Inhaled corticosteroid-related fracture risk adds to the concern about bone health. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 53"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with stable, long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a serum bicarbonate of 33 and an arterial carbon dioxide pressure of 54 mmHg on a blood gas. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Primary metabolic acidosis","Acute respiratory alkalosis","Laboratory error to be repeated","Compensated chronic hypercapnia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blood gases in this disease usually show chronic hypercapnia, with arterial carbon dioxide above 45 mmHg, and the metabolic panel may show a raised bicarbonate. The combination shows a compensatory metabolic alkalosis, as his 54 and 33 do. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.55 and a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 34% predicted. Which airflow grade fits?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With the ratio below 0.70, the grade comes from the post-bronchodilator 1-second volume: 34% sits in the 30% to 49% band, grade 3, severe. Grade 4 would need less than 30%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has 5 days of worsening breathlessness, and his usual white sputum has become larger in volume and yellow-green. He is alert with stable vital signs and has no history of resistant organisms or Pseudomonas. Which oral antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Doxycycline","Levofloxacin","Intravenous vancomycin","No antibiotic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibiotics are given when shortness of breath comes with increased sputum volume and purulence over baseline, as here. For an uncomplicated exacerbation, doxycycline, a macrolide, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or amoxicillin-clavulanate is considered. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is starting pulmonary rehabilitation and asks what it will involve. Which description is accurate?","choices":["A surgical procedure on the lungs","Breathing and secretion-clearing training","A home program done alone","Weekly antibiotics to prevent infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary rehabilitation covers clearing secretions, breathing techniques, strengthening exercises, energy conservation and avoiding fatigue, and patient education. It cannot be done alone; it needs a healthcare professional or interdisciplinary team. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man is recovering in the hospital from an exacerbation. He has been clinically stable for 18 hours, walks across the room, eats and sleeps without waking from breathlessness, and his home oxygen is arranged, but he still needs albuterol every 2 hours. Which finding delays his discharge?","choices":["Stable for 18 hours","Walking across the room","Home oxygen arranged","Albuterol needed every 2 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A discharge criterion is needing inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist no more often than every 4 hours. Every 2 hours means his airflow is not yet controlled enough for home, even though his other criteria are met. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is ready to quit smoking and asks for a medicine to help. He has tried quitting alone twice. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Varenicline with counseling","Varenicline without counseling","Tiotropium to ease cravings","Cutting down without a quit date"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every smoking cessation attempt should be combined with counseling, which looks for triggers and gives coping strategies, to increase efficacy. Varenicline, bupropion or nicotine replacement can be added; his failed solo attempts make the combined plan the better choice. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a daytime resting oxygen saturation of 94% and normal saturation while walking, but overnight oximetry shows saturations of 86% at night only. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Oxygen during exertion","No further evaluation","Evaluate for obstructive sleep apnea","Long-term oxygen over 15 hours a day"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Desaturation to 88% or less at night only calls for evaluation for obstructive sleep apnea and screening for other causes, rather than long-term oxygen. His daytime resting and exertional saturations are normal. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with group B chronic obstructive pulmonary disease still smokes a pack a day. She asks what single step would do the most to slow her disease. Which intervention is most appropriate?","choices":["A chest computed tomography scan","Smoking cessation with counseling","Adding an inhaled corticosteroid","Long-term azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation is the only treatment that can slow progression, and every attempt should be combined with counseling to increase efficacy; nicotine replacement, bupropion or varenicline can be added. It is essential for every group. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 43, 46"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with newly confirmed chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a symptom assessment test score of 6, a modified Medical Research Council dyspnea grade of 1, and no exacerbations in the past year. Which initial medication is most appropriate?","choices":["Daily oral corticosteroids","A single bronchodilator","Dual long-acting bronchodilators","An inhaled corticosteroid alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A score of 6 and a dyspnea grade of 1 mean few symptoms, and no exacerbations means low risk, so he is group A, which starts with 1 bronchodilator, short- or long-acting. He still needs smoking cessation if he smokes, and physical activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40, 49"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, few symptoms and no exacerbations has a routine follow-up visit. Which test is not routinely needed for him?","choices":["Spirometry over time","A symptom assessment test","Review of exacerbation history","Arterial blood gas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An arterial blood gas is usually done only in a deteriorating or severely exacerbated patient. A stable, low-symptom outpatient is followed with symptoms, exacerbation history and spirometry instead. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and osteoporosis takes triple inhaled therapy. She has had 2 pneumonias this year and a wrist fracture after a fall. Which component of her regimen is linked to both pneumonia and fracture risk?","choices":["The inhaled corticosteroid","The long-acting beta agonist","The long-acting antimuscarinic","Her as-needed albuterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled corticosteroids increase the risk of pneumonia, and in older adults the concerns include inhaled corticosteroid-related pneumonia and fracture risk. Her repeated pneumonias and fracture make that component the one to reassess. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 42, 53"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a resting oxygen saturation of 91% on room air, and she stays at 91% during a walking test. She asks for home oxygen. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["She needs a sleep study first","No long-term oxygen is indicated","She needs oxygen over 15 hours a day","She needs oxygen during exertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A resting saturation of 89% to 93% is moderate resting desaturation, with no indication for long-term oxygen, and her saturation stays at 91% with exertion. Oxygen is indicated with severe hypoxemia, an arterial oxygen pressure under 55 mmHg or saturation of 88% or less. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man is newly diagnosed with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 35% predicted. He has never had a pneumococcal vaccine. When should he receive it?","choices":["Now, at diagnosis","At age 65","After his first exacerbation","Once heart disease develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The pneumococcal vaccine is routine at 65, but patients with severe disease (1-second volume below 40%) or cardiac comorbidities should get it earlier, essentially when diagnosed and recognized at that severity. At 35% he qualifies now. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has 10 days of steadily worsening breathlessness and cough after a viral illness. Which term describes her presentation?","choices":["Disease progression","Stable group B disease","Acute bronchitis","Acute exacerbation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An acute exacerbation is an acute worsening of dyspnea, cough or sputum over 14 days or less, commonly triggered by viral or bacterial infection or pollution. Her 10-day decline after a viral illness fits, once pneumonia, embolism, acute heart failure and pneumothorax are excluded. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and few symptoms asks to be started on an inhaled corticosteroid by itself because a friend with asthma uses one. Which regimen should be avoided in him?","choices":["An inhaled corticosteroid alone","A single short-acting bronchodilator","Smoking cessation with counseling","A yearly influenza vaccine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In this disease, inhaled corticosteroids must be combined with a long-acting beta agonist, and then with a long-acting antimuscarinic as triple therapy, and cannot be used on their own. They also raise pneumonia risk. Alone, they are an asthma treatment. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 42"}]};
